    "active_paradigms": [
      {
        "idea": "Greedy prefix-extension ordering via incremental Trie simulation on serialized prefixes",
        "description": "Instead of scoring whole candidate orderings, build per-row orders greedily: maintain for each row the string serialized so far; at each step, for each remaining column, compute the marginal character-reuse gain of appending that column's value to each row's current prefix, measured as sum over value-groups of (rows sharing the same next-value) times len(value) \u2014 approximating Trie edge savings. Choose the column maximizing total gain, append it, and continue. This directly optimizes the actual Trie edge-count objective incrementally and adapts per group of rows sharing identical prefixes (rows diverge naturally, giving row-specific orders). Use precomputed factorized codes and bincount on the current prefix-group partition to keep each step O(p * n) vectorized.",
        "what_to_optimize": "exact serial Trie reuse (hit rate)",
        "cautions": "Track prefix-groups explicitly (rows with identical serialized prefix so far); when a group has distinct next values, splitting it lets different subgroups pick different next columns \u2014 this yields per-row orderings for free. Bound steps at p, use integer codes, avoid pandas in the loop. Compare final result against the current best via one LCP scoring pass and keep the better.",
        "approach_type": "greedy incremental trie-edge minimization"
      },
      {
        "idea": "Merge merged-column constraints into ordering space and exploit col_merge groups as atomic prefix blocks",
        "description": "The evaluator passes col_merge groups (e.g. movies: movieinfo+movietitle+rottentomatoeslink). Currently merging_columns is applied then ordering proceeds. Instead, treat each merge group as a unit: since the merged text is a concatenation, ordering columns inside the group still matters for the serialized string. Build candidates that place merge-group columns contiguously at the front (long shared text first) and vary internal order by frequency rank, giving 2-3 extra cheap candidates scored once each with the exact LCP sum. Also generate candidates that interleave: merge-group block first vs frequency-ranked block first.",
        "what_to_optimize": "hit rate on datasets with large merged text fields (movies, BIRD, PDMX)",
        "cautions": "Do not alter cell values; merging_columns must keep API semantics. Keep candidate count bounded (\u22643 extra) so runtime stays well under the 12s cap; dedupe by signature as now.",
        "approach_type": "candidate expansion with merge-aware blocks"
      },
      {
        "idea": "Suffix-aware tail ordering: order remaining columns by actual LCP contribution measured on a sample",
        "description": "After the tree/greedy front columns are fixed, the tail order is currently frequency-ranked heuristically. Replace with an empirical tail selection: for each leaf group, take a bounded sample of rows (e.g. \u22642000), serialize candidate tail orders, and compute the exact LCP sum for a few tail permutations (frequency order, savings order, length-descending order), picking the best per group. Since tail strings are short relative to the shared prefix, scoring is cheap. This is a bounded local search over tail permutations guided by the true objective rather than a proxy.",
        "what_to_optimize": "remaining reuse in tail columns after partitioning",
        "cautions": "Sample deterministically (e.g. first rows by code order) for reproducibility; cap total tail scorings per dataset (e.g. \u2264 50 LCP evaluations); reuse precomputed col_strs; skip entirely if n*p is huge to protect runtime.",
        "approach_type": "bounded local search over tail permutations"
      }
    ],
